.TH LLM-FIT 1 "2026-09" "llm-fit" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
llm-fit \- which LLMs this machine can run, and how fast
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B llm-fit
.I command
.RI [ flags ]
.RI [ argument ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B llm-fit
inspects the local hardware and reports which large language models fit in it,
at which quantization and on which runtime, with an estimated speed for each.
.PP
Speed figures are estimates derived from memory bandwidth and compute, not
measurements. They are usually within about 20% on hardware in the built-in
table; treat them as "this is the right ballpark" rather than a benchmark.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B detect
What hardware is here, and what it implies.
.TP
.B suggest
Models that run well, best first.
.TP
.BI check " model"
Every quantization and runtime for one model.
.TP
.BI check " file.gguf"
Read the shape and quantization from a local GGUF file.
.TP
.B engines
Runtime support matrix for this machine.
.TP
.B models
The built-in catalogue.
.TP
.BR validate " [\fIFILE\fR]"
Compare the tool's own estimates against measured runs (default
.IR bench/observations.tsv ).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-ctx " N"
Context length to plan for (default 8192).
.TP
.BI \-kv " TYPE"
KV cache precision:
.BR f16 ", " q8_0 ", " q4_0
(default f16).
.TP
.BI \-batch " N"
Concurrent sequences (default 1).
.TP
.BI \-engine " NAME"
Restrict to one runtime.
.TP
.B \-serving
Optimise for concurrent throughput rather than chat latency.
.TP
.BI \-min " LEVEL"
.BR unusable | sluggish | usable | good | excellent
(default usable).
.TP
.BI \-min\-quality " N"
Quantization quality floor, 0\-100 (default 55: no sub-3-bit).
.TP
.BI \-top " N"
How many to list (default 12).
.TP
.B \-hf
Read the architecture from Hugging Face instead of the built-in catalogue, e.g.
.BR "llm-fit check \-hf Qwen/Qwen3-14B" .
.TP
.B \-json
Machine-readable output.
.TP
.BI \-gpu " NAME"
Plan for a different card ("RTX 4090", "A100 80GB", "M4 Max"), taking its VRAM,
bandwidth and compute from the spec table.
.TP
.BI \-vram " GiB"
Override VRAM only, keeping the detected bandwidth.
.TP
.BI \-ram " GiB"
Override detected system RAM.
.TP
.BI \-ram\-bandwidth " GBs"
Override system RAM bandwidth.
.TP
.BI \-parallel " tensor|layer"
How to spread across several GPUs. The default is what the chosen runtime
actually does: llama.cpp splits layers (extra cards buy capacity), while
vLLM, SGLang, ExLlamaV2 and TensorRT-LLM shard tensors (extra cards buy
speed). Ignored with one GPU.
.TP
.BI \-interconnect " GBs"
GPU link bandwidth for tensor parallelism (default 32, one direction of
PCIe 4.0 x16; NVLink is ~300+).
.TP
.BI \-draft " MODEL"
Pair a speculative draft model with the target.
.TP
.BI \-lookahead " N"
Tokens the draft proposes per verification step (default 4).
.TP
.BI \-acceptance " R"
Assumed acceptance rate, 0\-1 (default 0.7). It is reported in the output,
because the speedup depends on it more than on anything else.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 64
Usage error: an unknown verb or a bad flag.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
What this machine is, and what fits:
.PP
.RS
.B llm-fit detect
.br
.B llm-fit suggest \-ctx 32768
.RE
.PP
One model, every quantization:
.PP
.RS
.B llm-fit check llama-3.1-8b
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
Project documentation:
.UR https://github.com/fabiocicerchia/llm-fit
.UE
